---
import { astraPreview } from "../data/manuscripts";
---
<article class="publication-preview" id={astraPreview.id} aria-labelledby="astra-title">
  <div class="publication-banner publication-banner--stacked">
    <header class="preview-heading">
      <h2 id="astra-title">{astraPreview.title}</h2>
      <p class="preview-description">{astraPreview.description}</p>
      <div class="dm-meta-row" style="margin-top: 18px;">
        <span>2026</span>
        <span>{astraPreview.availability}</span>
        <a class="dm-meta-link" href={astraPreview.arxiv}>arXiv <span class="dm-chevron" aria-hidden="true">›</span></a>
        <a class="dm-meta-link" href={astraPreview.pdf}>PDF <span class="dm-chevron" aria-hidden="true">›</span></a>
      </div>
    </header>
    <figure class="preview-figure preview-figure--wide">
      <a href="/figures/astra/scorecard-c.webp" aria-label="View the scorecard at full size">
        <img src="/figures/astra/scorecard-c.webp" width="1800" height="465" loading="eager" decoding="async"
          alt="Scorecard: the CRPS of Astra is lower than that of the ECMWF ensemble, IFS ENS, for all eight variables at all 30 lead times from 12 hours to 15 days, by up to about 30% at short lead times; every cell is blue and significant."/>
      </a>
      <figcaption>CRPS improvement over the ECMWF ensemble (IFS ENS) in WeatherBench 2 at 0.25°: lower on every variable and lead time.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <details class="geometry-figure" data-geometry-figure>
    <summary class="geometry-summary" id="ka-title">Kantorovich Attention in motion</summary>
    <div class="geometry-heading">
      <p>Each region (tile) of the grid chooses both the tiles it reads from (pull) and the tiles it writes to (push). Together the two choices form a transport plan in which every tile sends and receives at token resolution.</p>
      <button class="motion-toggle" type="button" hidden data-motion-toggle>Play animation</button>
    </div>
    <ul class="ka-points">
      <li><strong class="ka-pull">Pull</strong> Each tile picks the <em>K</em> tiles it reads from. Alone, it leaves some tiles unread (60 of 180 on the trained 2° globe).</li>
      <li><strong class="ka-push">Push</strong> Each tile picks the <em>K</em> tiles it writes to, so every tile is read by at least <em>K</em> others.</li>
      <li><strong class="ka-plan">Two-sided plan</strong> Without a linear-programming solver, the union of pull and push leaves 0 unread tiles by construction and reaches 94–98% of the optimal transport plan’s gain.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="geometry-panels">
      <figure>
        <h3><span aria-hidden="true">a.</span> Two-sided 3D tile routing</h3>
        <img
          src="/figures/astra/ka-routing-still.png"
          data-still-src="/figures/astra/ka-routing-still.png"
          data-animation-src="/figures/astra/ka-routing.gif"
          width="720" height="518" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
          alt="Animation cycling through Pull alone (leaving 2 senders unread), Push alone (leaving 2 receivers reading nothing), and Kantorovich Attention (Pull union Push) on 3D raised Earth plates with twisting transport tubes."
        />
        <figcaption>Pull gathers <em>K</em> senders into query tile <em>i</em>; push scatters <em>i</em> to <em>K</em> receivers. Their union satisfies both marginal floors (≥ <em>K</em>) and achieves total score 21.8 vs. the LP optimum 21.9.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <h3><span aria-hidden="true">b.</span> Global coverage on trained scores</h3>
        <img
          src="/figures/astra/ka-coverage-still.png"
          data-still-src="/figures/astra/ka-coverage-still.png"
          data-animation-src="/figures/astra/ka-coverage.gif"
          width="720" height="518" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
          alt="Animation of the 180 global 20-degree raised Earth tiles on a trained L2 attention head: one-sided Pull leaves 60 of 180 tiles unread (hatched), whereas Two-sided top-K rescues every unread tile (0 unread by construction)."
        />
        <figcaption>Across 4,608 trained score matrices, one-sided pull leaves 28–38% of global tiles unread. Two-sided top-<em>K</em> guarantees 0% unread tiles by construction and captures 94–98% of the optimal transport gain.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <figure class="preview-figure ka-figure" style="margin-top: 28px;">
      <a href="/figures/astra/figure-2.webp" aria-label="View Figure 2 at full size">
        <img src="/figures/astra/figure-2.webp" width="2237" height="2132" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
          alt="Figure 2: a, one tile at the top of a cut-away Earth reads from, writes to, and exchanges with selected tiles through transport tubes, while every other tile reaches it through a pooled term. b, plans on the trained network's scores: pull alone leaves 60 of 180 tiles unread, the two-sided top-K plan none, close to the optimal plan. c, a toy plan with at least K partners per tile on both sides."/>
      </a>
      <figcaption>Figure 2. Full architecture overview: 3D cut-away globe (a), trained-network score plans (b), and bipartite 3D plate decomposition (c).</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </details>
</article>
